{"type":"diagram","design":"S66_ARCH_NUMBERED","title":"Current architecture and its problems in a single region","groups":[{"label":"Single Compute node","items":[{"title":"Standalone SQL Server VM","text":"Single-zone Compute Engine deployment","icon":"database"},{"title":"Direct IP connections","text":"No internal load balancer routing","icon":"network-hub"}]},{"label":"Attached block storage","items":[{"title":"Single persistent disk pool","text":"Shared volume for data and logs","icon":"cloud-database"}]},{"label":"Regional network","items":[{"title":"Single-region VPC subnet","text":"Isolated to one cloud region","icon":"cloud-wifi"}]},{"label":"Disaster recovery posture","items":[{"title":"Snapshot-only backups","text":"No standby SQL Server replica","icon":"cloud-download"}]}],"callouts":["Single Compute Engine SQL Server creates a single point of failure for workloads.","Storage exhaustion on Persistent Disks causes total outage without replica isolation.","Instance failure or regional network isolation halts all clinical database operations across Contoso Health.","No secondary Google Cloud region for disaster recovery forces manual snapshot restores."],"notes":"Contoso Health currently relies on a single-instance SQL Server deployment in one region, which presents a single point of failure where storage exhaustion, instance failure, or regional network isolation causes total service failure. Without a secondary Google Cloud region or standby replica, recovery requires manual intervention.

