{"type":"table","design":"S66_TABLE_NOTES","title":"Configuration and sizing","lead":"Standardized component settings govern clustering, replication modes, and storage across both regions.","columns":["Component layer","Primary region setting","Secondary DR setting","Availability role"],"rows":[["Compute Engine nodes","2 memory-optimized VMs across zones","1 memory-optimized VM in standby zone","Symmetrical compute capacity for failover"],["Always On replication","Synchronous commit with automatic failover","Asynchronous commit with manual failover","Zero local data loss and insulated latency"],["Traffic routing","Internal Load Balancer or DNN listener","Standby Internal Load Balancer and Cloud DNS","Routes connections without gratuitous ARP"],["Disk subsystem","Isolated PD-SSD for OS, data, logs; Local SSD tempdb","Identical PD-SSD and Local SSD layout","Prevents I/O bottlenecks during peak load"],["Cluster quorum","Node majority with 1 vote per local replica","0 vote async replica; 3rd-region witness","Prevents split-brain during network partition"]],"tips":[{"title":"Listener routing on Compute Engine","points":["VPC networks do not broadcast gratuitous ARP requests on failover","Internal Load Balancers probe active replicas to direct client connections"]},{"title":"Read scale-out ceiling","points":["Always On architecture supports up to eight secondary replicas if reporting offload is enabled later","Keep the DR replica strictly passive to preserve zero-cost Software Assurance failover licensing"]}],"notes":"This specification table summarizes how Compute Engine instances, storage volumes, and Always On Availability Groups are configured across the primary and DR regions. Using Internal Load Balancers or Distributed Network Name listeners resolves the lack of gratuitous ARP support in cloud VPC networks while enabling rapid failover routing.

