{"type":"diagram","design":"S66_REGION_TOPOLOGY","title":"Target architecture across two Google Cloud regions","regions":[{"label":"Primary region (active HA)","zones":[{"title":"Zone A primary","text":"Active SQL Server"},{"title":"Zone B secondary","text":"Synchronous standby HA"}],"detail":"Synchronous commit Always On AG with regional Internal Load Balancer"},{"label":"Secondary DR region (passive)","zones":[{"title":"Zone A DR standby","text":"Async standby SQL node"},{"title":"Zone B infrastructure","text":"Standby witness subnet"}],"detail":"Asynchronous commit replica with passive Internal Load Balancer"}],"link":"Async Always On AG peering","note":"A single Windows Server Failover Cluster spans both Google Cloud regions. Synchronous commit replication across two primary zones protects against zonal failures, while asynchronous commit replication to the secondary DR region prevents cross-region network latency from slowing primary clinical writes.","notes":"The target architecture follows Google Cloud best practices by deploying a multi-regional Always On Availability Group over Windows Server Failover Clustering (WSFC). Within the primary region, synchronous commit replication across two Compute Engine zones provides local high availability, while asynchronous commit replication to the secondary Google Cloud region provides disaster recovery without impacting primary transaction latency.

